#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:1
msgid "CLI guideline"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:3
msgid "Goals"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:5
msgid ""
"Purpose of this document is to provide a clear direction to **help design "
"delightful command line** experience. This document contains guidelines to "
"follow to ensure a consistent and approachable user experience."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:9
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:11
msgid ""
"`nix` command provides a single entry to a number of sub-commands that help "
"**developers and system administrators** in the life-cycle of a software "
"project. We particularly need to pay special attention to help and assist "
"new users of Nix."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:16
msgid "Naming the `COMMANDS`"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:18
msgid ""
"Words matter. Naming is an important part of the usability. Users will be "
"interacting with Nix on a regular basis so we should **name things for ease "
"of understanding**."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:22
msgid ""
"We recommend following the [Principle of Least Astonishment](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principle_of_least_astonishment). This means that you "
"should **never use acronyms or abbreviations** unless they are commonly used "
"in other tools (e.g. `nix init`). And if the command name is too long (> "
"10-12 characters) then shortening it makes sense (e.g. “prioritization” → "
"“priority”)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:29
msgid ""
"Commands should **follow a noun-verb dialogue**. Although noun-verb "
"formatting seems backwards from a speaking perspective (i.e. `nix store "
"copy` vs. `nix copy store`) it allows us to organize commands the same way "
"users think about completing an action (the group first, then the command)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:34
msgid "Naming rules"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:36
msgid ""
"Rules are there to guide you by limiting your options. But not everything "
"can fit the rules all the time. In those cases document the exceptions in "
"[Appendix 1: Commands naming exceptions](#appendix-1-commands-naming-"
"exceptions) and provide reason. The rules want to force a Nix developer to "
"look, not just at the command at hand, but also the command in a full "
"context alongside other `nix` commands."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:47
msgid ""
"`GROUP`, `COMMAND`, `ARGUMENTS` and `OPTIONS` should be lowercase and in a "
"singular form."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:49
msgid "`GROUP` should be a  **NOUN**."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:50
msgid "`COMMAND` should be a **VERB**."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:51
msgid "`ARGUMENTS` and `OPTIONS` are discussed in [_Input_ section](#input)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:53
msgid "Classification"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:55
msgid ""
"Some commands are more important, some less. While we want all of our "
"commands to be perfect we can only spend limited amount of time testing and "
"improving them."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:59
msgid ""
"This classification tries to separate commands in 3 categories in terms of "
"their importance in regards to the new users. Users who are likely to be "
"impacted the most by bad user experience."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:63
msgid "**Main commands**"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:65
msgid ""
"Commands used for our main use cases and most likely used by new users. We "
"expect attention to details, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:68
#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:86
msgid ""
"Proper use of [colors](#colors), [emojis](#special-unicode-characters) and "
"[aligning of text](#text-alignment)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:70
#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:88
#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:97
msgid "[Autocomplete](#shell-completion) of options."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:71
msgid "Show [next possible steps](#next-steps)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:72
msgid ""
"Showing some [“tips”](#educate-the-user) when running logs running tasks "
"(eg. building / downloading) in order to teach users interesting bits of Nix "
"ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:75
msgid ""
"[Help pages](#help-is-essential) to be as good as we can write them pointing "
"to external documentation and tutorials for more."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:78
msgid ""
"Examples of such commands: `nix init`, `nix develop`, `nix build`, `nix "
"run`, ..."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:81
msgid "**Infrequently used commands**"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:83
msgid ""
"From infrequently used commands we expect less attention to details, but "
"still some:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:90
msgid "Examples of such commands: `nix edit`, `nix eval`, ..."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:92
msgid "**Utility and scripting commands**"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:94
msgid ""
"Commands that expose certain internal functionality of `nix`, mostly used by "
"other scripts."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:99
msgid ""
"Examples of such commands: `nix store copy`, `nix hash base16`, `nix store "
"ping`, ..."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:103
msgid "Help is essential"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:105
msgid ""
"Help should be built into your command line so that new users can gradually "
"discover new features when they need them."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:108
msgid "Looking for help"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:110
msgid ""
"Since there is no standard way how user will look for help we rely on ways "
"help is provided by commonly used tools. As a guide for this we took `git` "
"and whenever in doubt look at it as a preferred direction."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:114
msgid "The rules are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:116
msgid ""
"Help is shown by using `--help` or `help` command (eg `nix` ```--``help``` "
"or `nix help`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:118
msgid ""
"For non-COMMANDs (eg. `nix` ```--``help``` and `nix store` ```--``help```) "
"we **show a summary** of most common use cases. Summary is presented on the "
"STDOUT without any use of PAGER."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:121
msgid ""
"For COMMANDs (eg. `nix init` ```--``help``` or `nix help init`) we display "
"the man page of that command. By default the PAGER is used (as in `git`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:123
msgid ""
"At the end of either summary or man page there should be an URL pointing to "
"an online version of more detailed documentation."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:125
msgid ""
"The structure of summaries and man pages should be the same as in `git`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:127
msgid "Anticipate where help is needed"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:129
msgid ""
"Even better then requiring the user to search for help is to anticipate and "
"predict when user might need it. Either because the lack of discoverability, "
"typo in the input or simply taking the opportunity to teach the user of "
"interesting - but less visible - details."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:134
msgid "Shell completion"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:136
msgid ""
"This type of help is most common and almost expected by users. We need to "
"**provide the best shell completion** for `bash`, `zsh` and `fish`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:139
msgid "Completion needs to be **context aware**, this mean when a user types:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:145
msgid "we need to display a list of flakes starting with `n`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:147
msgid "Wrong input"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:149
msgid ""
"As we all know we humans make mistakes, all the time. When a typo - "
"intentional or unintentional - is made, we should prompt for closest "
"possible options or point to the documentation which would educate user to "
"not make the same errors. Here are few examples:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:154
msgid "In first example we prompt the user for typing wrong command name:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:167
msgid ""
"Sometimes users will make mistake either because of a typo or simply because "
"of lack of discoverability. Our handling of this cases needs to be context "
"sensitive."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:184
msgid "Next steps"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:186
msgid ""
"It can be invaluable to newcomers to show what a possible next steps and "
"what is the usual development workflow with Nix. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:200
msgid "Educate the user"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:202
msgid ""
"We should take any opportunity to **educate users**, but at the same time we "
"must **be very very careful to not annoy users**. There is a thin line "
"between being helpful and being annoying."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:206
msgid ""
"An example of educating users might be to provide _Tips_ in places where "
"they are waiting."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:229
msgid ""
"Now **Learn** part of the output is where you educate users. You should only "
"show it when you know that a build will take some time and not annoy users "
"of the builds that take only few seconds."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:233
msgid ""
"Every feature like this should go through an intensive review and testing to "
"collect as much feedback as possible and to fine tune every little detail. "
"If done right this can be an awesome features beginners and advance users "
"will love, but if not done perfectly it will annoy users and leave bad "
"impression."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:238
msgid "Input"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:240
msgid "Input to a command is provided via `ARGUMENTS` and `OPTIONS`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:242
msgid ""
"`ARGUMENTS` represent a required input for a function. When choosing to use "
"`ARGUMENTS` over `OPTIONS` please be aware of the downsides that come with "
"it:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:245
msgid ""
"User will need to remember the order of `ARGUMENTS`. This is not a problem "
"if there is only one `ARGUMENT`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:247
msgid "With `OPTIONS` it is possible to provide much better auto completion."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:248
msgid "With `OPTIONS` it is possible to provide much better error message."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:249
msgid "Using `OPTIONS` it will mean there is a little bit more typing."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:251
msgid ""
"We don’t discourage the use of `ARGUMENTS`, but simply want to make every "
"developer consider the downsides and choose wisely."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:254
msgid "Naming the `OPTIONS`"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:256
msgid ""
"The only naming convention - apart from the ones mentioned in Naming the "
"`COMMANDS` section is how flags are named."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:259
msgid ""
"Flags are a type of `OPTION` that represent an option that can be turned ON "
"of OFF. We can say **flags are boolean type of** `**OPTION**`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:262
msgid "Here are few examples of flag `OPTIONS`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:264
msgid "`--colors` vs. `--no-colors` (showing colors in the output)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:265
msgid "`--emojis` vs. `--no-emojis` (showing emojis in the output)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:267
msgid "Prompt when input not provided"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:269
msgid ""
"For _main commands_ (as [per classification](#classification)) we want "
"command to improve the discoverability of possible input. A new user will "
"most likely not know which `ARGUMENTS` and `OPTIONS` are required or which "
"values are possible for those options."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:274
msgid ""
"In case the user does not provide the input or they provide wrong input, "
"rather than show the error, prompt a user with an option to find and select "
"correct input (see examples)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:278
msgid ""
"Prompting is of course not required when TTY is not attached to STDIN. This "
"would mean that scripts won't need to handle prompt, but rather handle "
"errors."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:281
msgid "A place to use prompt and provide user with interactive select"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:294
msgid ""
"Another great place to add prompts are **confirmation dialogues for "
"dangerous actions**. For example when adding new substitutor via `OPTIONS` "
"or via `flake.nix` we should prompt - for the first time - and let user "
"review what is going to happen."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:300
#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:553
msgid ""
"```shell\n"
"$ nix build --option substitutors https://cache.example.org\n"
"------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
"  Warning! A security related question needs to be answered.\n"
"------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
"  The following substitutors will be used to in `my-project`:\n"
"    - https://cache.example.org\n"
"\n"
"  Do you allow `my-project` to use above mentioned substitutors?\n"
"    [y/N] |> y\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:312
msgid "Output"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:314
msgid ""
"Terminal output can be quite limiting in many ways. Which should force us to "
"think about the experience even more. As with every design the output is a "
"compromise between being terse and being verbose, between showing help to "
"beginners and annoying advance users. For this it is important that we know "
"what are the priorities."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:320
msgid ""
"Nix command line should be first and foremost written with beginners in "
"mind. But users won't stay beginners for long and what was once useful might "
"quickly become annoying. There is no golden rule that we can give in this "
"guideline that would make it easier how to draw a line and find best "
"compromise."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:325
msgid ""
"What we would encourage is to **build prototypes**, do some **user testing** "
"and collect **feedback**. Then repeat the cycle few times."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:328
msgid ""
"First design the _happy path_ and only after your iron it out, continue to "
"work on **edge cases** (handling and displaying errors, changes of the "
"output by certain `OPTIONS`, etc…)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:332
msgid "Follow best practices"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:334
msgid ""
"Needless to say we Nix must be a good citizen and follow best practices in "
"command line."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:337
msgid "In short: **STDOUT is for output, STDERR is for (human) messaging.**"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:339
msgid ""
"STDOUT and STDERR provide a way for you to output messages to the user while "
"also allowing them to redirect content to a file. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:342
msgid ""
"```shell\n"
"$ nix build > build.txt\n"
"------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
"  Error! Attribute `bin` missing at (1:94) from string.\n"
"------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
"\n"
"  1| with import <nixpkgs> { }; (pkgs.runCommandCC or pkgs.runCommand) "
"\"shell\" { buildInputs = [ (surge.bin) ]; } \"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:351
msgid "Because this warning is on STDERR, it doesn’t end up in the file."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:353
msgid ""
"But not everything on STDERR is an error though. For example, you can run "
"`nix build` and collect logs in a file while still seeing the progress."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:376
msgid "Errors (WIP)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:378
msgid ""
"**TODO**: Once we have implementation for the _happy path_ then we will "
"think how to present errors."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:381
msgid "Not only for humans"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:383
msgid ""
"Terse, machine-readable output formats can also be useful but shouldn’t get "
"in the way of making beautiful CLI output. When needed, commands should "
"offer a `--json` flag to allow users to easily parse and script the CLI."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:387
msgid ""
"When TTY is not detected on STDOUT we should remove all design elements (no "
"colors, no emojis and using ASCII instead of Unicode symbols). The same "
"should happen when TTY is not detected on STDERR. We should not display "
"progress / status section, but only print warnings and errors."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:392
msgid "Dialog with the user"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:394
msgid ""
"CLIs don't always make it clear when an action has taken place. For every "
"action a user performs, your CLI should provide an equal and appropriate "
"reaction, clearly highlighting the what just happened. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:407
msgid ""
"Above command clearly states that command successfully completed. And in "
"case of `nix build`, which is a command that might take some time to "
"complete, it is equally important to also show that a command started."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:411
msgid "Text alignment"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:413
msgid ""
"Text alignment is the number one design element that will present all of the "
"Nix commands as a family and not as separate tools glued together."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:416
msgid "The format we should follow is:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:425
msgid "Few rules that we can extract from above example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:427
msgid "Each line should start at least with one space."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:428
msgid "First word should be a VERB and must be aligned to the right."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:429
msgid "Second word should be a NOUN and must be aligned to the left."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:430
msgid ""
"If you can not find a good VERB / NOUN pair, don’t worry make it as "
"understandable to the user as possible."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:432
msgid ""
"More details of each line can be provided by `|>` character which is serving "
"as the first word when aligning the text"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:435
msgid ""
"Don’t forget you should also test your terminal output with colors and "
"emojis off (`--no-colors --no-emojis`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:438
msgid "Dim / Bright"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:440
msgid ""
"After comparing few terminals with different color schemes we would "
"**recommend to avoid using dimmed text**. The difference from the rest of "
"the text is very little in many terminal and color scheme combinations. "
"Sometimes the difference is not even notable, therefore relying on it "
"wouldn’t make much sense."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:445
msgid ""
"**The bright text is much better supported** across terminals and color "
"schemes. Most of the time the difference is perceived as if the bright text "
"would be bold."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:449
msgid "Colors"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:451
msgid ""
"Humans are already conditioned by society to attach certain meaning to "
"certain colors. While the meaning is not universal, a simple collection of "
"colors is used to represent basic emotions."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:455
msgid "Colors that can be used in output"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:457
msgid "Red = error, danger, stop"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:458
msgid "Green = success, good"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:459
msgid "Yellow/Orange = proceed with caution, warning, in progress"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:460
msgid "Blue/Magenta = stability, calm"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:462
msgid ""
"While colors are nice, when command line is used by machines (in automation "
"scripts) you want to remove the colors. There should be a global `--no-"
"colors` option that would remove the colors."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:466
msgid "Special (Unicode) characters"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:468
msgid ""
"Most of the terminal have good support for Unicode characters and you should "
"use them in your output by default. But always have a backup solution that "
"is implemented only with ASCII characters and will be used when `--ascii` "
"option is going to be passed in. Please make sure that you test your output "
"also without Unicode characters"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:474
msgid ""
"More they showing all the different Unicode characters it is important to "
"**establish common set of characters** that we use for certain situations."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:477
msgid "Emojis"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:479
msgid ""
"Emojis help channel emotions even better than text, colors and special "
"characters."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:482
msgid ""
"We recommend **keeping the set of emojis to a minimum**. This will enable "
"each emoji to stand out more."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:485
msgid ""
"As not everybody is happy about emojis we should provide an `--no-emojis` "
"option to disable them. Please make sure that you test your output also "
"without emojis."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:489
msgid "Tables"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:491
msgid ""
"All commands that are listing certain data can be implemented in some sort "
"of a table. It’s important that each row of your output is a single ‘entry’ "
"of data. Never output table borders. It’s noisy and a huge pain for parsing "
"using other tools such as `grep`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:496
msgid ""
"Be mindful of the screen width. Only show a few columns by default with the "
"table header, for more the table can be manipulated by the following options:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:499
msgid "`--no-headers`: Show column headers by default but allow to hide them."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:500
msgid "`--columns`: Comma-separated list of column names to add."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:501
msgid ""
"`--sort`: Allow sorting by column. Allow inverse and multi-column sort as "
"well."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:503
msgid "Interactive output"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:505
msgid ""
"Interactive output was selected to be able to strike the balance between "
"beginners and advance users. While the default output will target beginners "
"it can, with a few key strokes, be changed into and advance introspection "
"tool."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:509
msgid "Progress"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:511
msgid ""
"For longer running commands we should provide and overview the progress. "
"This is shown best in `nix build` example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:534
msgid "Search"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:536
msgid ""
"Use a `fzf` like fuzzy search when there are multiple options to choose from."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:548
msgid "Prompt"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:550
msgid ""
"In some situations we need to prompt the user and inform the user about what "
"is going to happen."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:565
msgid "Verbosity"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:567
msgid "There are many ways that you can control verbosity."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:569
msgid "Verbosity levels are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:571
msgid "`ERROR` (level 0)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:572
msgid "`WARN` (level 1)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:573
msgid "`NOTICE` (level 2)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:574
msgid "`INFO` (level 3)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:575
msgid "`TALKATIVE` (level 4)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/cli-guideline.md:576
msgid "`CHATTY` (level 5)"
